{"title":"Royal Women Celebrating Diwali","artist":"Artist not identified","date":"c. 1760","medium":"gum tempera and gold on paper","classification":"Painting","styles":[],"subjects":[],"origin":"Northern India, Uttar Pradesh, Lucknow","museum_caption":"Lined along the eaves, the top of the marble lattice railing, and the rims of the boats on the river are candles and butter lamps lit in celebration of a New Year festival. A princess on a golden armchair lights sparklers with her friends. In the boats and on the far shore men set off sparklers under the light of a magnificent firework display under the full moon. The style of this work is typical of Mughal painting from the mid-1700s, when scenes of domestic life among women of the court were a favorite subject for the imperial artists. Did you know? \"Diwali\" comes from the Sanskrit words dīpa (lamp) and āvali (rows or series)."}

