{"title":"Pendant of a Bird (Rooster?)","artist":"Artist not identified","date":"700s–600s BCE","medium":"bronze","classification":"Jewelry","styles":[],"subjects":[],"origin":"Northern Greece, Macedonian, Geometric period  (900-700 BCE)","museum_caption":"Although quite schematic, the subject of this pendant is easily recognizable as a bird, likely a rooster. Cleverly designed to hang evenly from its central suspension loop, it comprises three main parts: long neck terminating in a head with a pointy beak and small crest; triangular body with stylized feet; and broadly arched tail feathers. Did you know? The green patina of this bronze bird pendant is a result of oxidation."}

