{"title":"Clouds and Waves at the Wu Gorge","artist":"Xie Shichen (Chinese, 1487–after 1567)","date":"c. 1547–67","medium":"hanging scroll; ink and light color on paper","classification":"Painting","styles":[],"subjects":[],"origin":"China, Ming dynasty (1368–1644)","museum_caption":"This dramatic landscape was probably painted after Xie Shichen returned from a journey in 1547 that took him through various provinces of China, including Sichuan Province in southwest China and sailing the Yangzi River. The composition from a high vantage point offers a view deep into the Wu Gorge where rolling waves put a ship in peril. The scale and composition of this landscape, which was inspired by the majestic mountain scenery in Sichuan Province, as well as Xie’s vigorous brushwork, are exceptional. Did you know? This large painting was likely created for a wealthy or powerful family's grand hall to impress their visitors."}

