{"title":"Breton Landscape","artist":"Jan Verkade (Dutch, 1868–1946)","date":"1891–92","medium":"oil on canvas","classification":"Painting","styles":[],"subjects":[],"origin":"Netherlands","museum_caption":"Brittany, in northwestern France, was an important place to Verkade both artistically and spiritually. The artist was captivated by the terrain that differed so greatly from that of Amsterdam, inspiring a series of Breton landscapes. Verkade also converted to Catholicism during his travels to Brittany, shifting the trajectory of his art away from landscapes and toward a revival of religious painting and sacred art. Did you know? Verkade painted many landscapes, including a drop cloth he painted for a puppet show titled Les Sept Princesses (The Seven Princesses)."}

